From 39adffa6facafe1c20e536efb011d73310554a3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 23:28:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 27/30] grate: keep values alive across fetches, add SNE and
 RCP, drop dead code

The fragment compiler lost values in several ways, found with a ladder of
small shaders around a texture fetch (tools/gpu/grate-tex-flag-test.c):

* A varying read after a texture fetch gave tile-constant garbage: the
  fetch result was parked in a global register across the interpolation.
  Any value that is alive across a fetch or an interpolation of a program
  with a fetch now lives in R0-R3, as a whole row or, for fetch results
  and packed values, one half of a row (allocate_coordinate_row()).
* SNE was unimplemented. "CMP(-|SNE(a, b)|, x, y)" becomes the SEQ with the
  two values swapped; any other use is SEQ followed by 1 - x.
* RCP is implemented with the SFU. The operand goes through a free row, the
  result comes back through the MFU multipliers straight into the
  destination row, or into a free row and a copy. A varying operand is
  interpolated first.
* "MOV t.xy, IN[k].xy; TEX t, t.xy" samples IN[k] directly.
* Instructions whose results nothing reads are dropped, and so are the
  unused channels of partly dead results, before registers are allocated.

R4 and up and the globals 16-23 are storage of four lanes (x mod 4), shared
by every pixel the sequencer has in flight. With MAX_OUT 0x12c pixels
overwrite each other's values, with 8 neighbouring pixels share one, and
only with MAX_OUT <= 4 does every pixel keep its own. A program that needs
a global register is now compiled with MAX_OUT 4 / MIN_OUT 2 (several times
slower per pixel); a program that stays in R0-R3 keeps the old setup.
